WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:05.840
Hey everyone, Jared here, 
one of the creators here at Wisecrack.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:10.580
Today we’ve got another special episode about a show that everyone is excited to see come back on the air:

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:12.020
The Walking Dead

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:15.920
The Walking Dead is the tale of sheriff Rick Grimes and his small band of survivors

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.380
as they're transformed from coddled complainers

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:21.100
Carol: "My daughter doesn't deserve to die like this!"

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:24.380
into battle-tested zombie murdering badasses.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:28.820
It’s also a show about British actor Andrew
Lincoln’s struggle to pronounce the name "Carl."

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.430
Rick: "Certainly not in front of 'Coral.'"

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:30.970
Rick: "Corel!"

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:31.750
Rick: "Koral!"

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:32.610
Rick: "Corral"

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:33.210
Rick: "Corol"

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:34.350
Rick: "Quuraaal!"

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:35.210
Rick: "Corall?!"

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:36.350
Rick: "Cural's gonna be alright"

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:38.160
Rick: "Cural"
Carl: "I got it!"

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:43.220
The zombie sub-genre has a rich history of social commentary, and the Walking Dead is no different.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:48.920
Whether they be the slow walking, brain craving type or of the fast running, shrieking persuasion,

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:53.360
the figure of the zombie has been a metaphor for all sorts of things that keep us up at night.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.240
Zombies have represented everything from

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:58.220
mindless consumers under capitalism in the Dawn of the Dead

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:01.620
To fears about public health crises in 28 Days Later

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.440
Immigration in World War Z

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:05.800
Or mega-corporations in Resident Evil.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:08.760
And then there’s the fact that zombies originated in Haiti,

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:11.440
where many have argued it was a metaphor for slavery.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:17.340
Zombies are projections of our own societal fears. 
But The Walking Dead isn’t quite any of these.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:22.360
Sure, it draws some parallels to predecessors, Such as when Andrea picks out a necklace for her sister

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:27.900
while a horde of zombies clamor to eat her, similar to the Dawn of the Dead where survivors take refuge in a

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:32.100
shopping mall and epitomize mindless consumers, while another kind of mindless consumer, while another kind of

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:38.480
mindless consumer eagerly awaits the opportunity to indulge in some organic, mall-to-table meat.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:44.760
Instead, The Walking Dead explores a multitude of issues like politics, psychology, and our relationship to death.

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:46.740
Also, the joys of cosplaying.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:48.340
Welcome to this special episode on

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:50.100
The Philosophy of The Walking Dead

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.480
Part 1: What does it mean to Live?

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.380
Why are zombies so scary?

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.620
It’s not just the fact that getting eaten alive 
probably sucks

00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:01.940
but our revulsion at zombies provides
deep insights into how we view our own lives.

00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:06.260
After all, plenty of things can rip out our intestines and, instead of inspiring terror,

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.660
inspire the dulcet tones of Werner Herzog.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:12.240
Herzog: "I see only the overwhelming indifference of nature."

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:16.160
Zombies keep us up at night because they’re like us, but not quite us.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:20.580
They also mess with the fundamental reason that we use to tell ourselves that we’re special.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:25.860
The Walking Dead is, above all else, a show
about philosophical boundaries, and 3 in particular:

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:27.020
What constitutes life?

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:28.220
What constitutes “living”?

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:29.940
And what constitutes being human?

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:32.000
Pharrell: ♫"These blurred lineeesss~"♫

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:36.360
The show is constantly asking us to interrogate the difference between life and death.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:40.420
It is after all, called "The Walking Dead," and they’re not just talking about the zombies —

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:42.040
They’re talking about the survivors.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:44.740
It raises the question: What makes us alive?

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:48.780
One of the best moments of the show that explores
this happens early on, at the CDC.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:50.420
Rick: "But they're not alive."

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:51.320
Dr. Jenner: "You tell me."

00:02:51.320 --> 00:02:54.200
When it’s revealed by Dr. Jenner that everyone is infected

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.280
all of the sudden, the line between “walker” 
and human seems a bit blurred.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:04.100
Not only will everyone reanimate, but they’re always so close to death that the distinction seems to melt away.

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:05.400
Clara: "Please help me."

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:10.320
When they open the door to the CDC, Rick’s group is blinded by a flood of light, clearly evoking

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:15.160
“the light at the end of the tunnel” to heaven, or whatever it is that people believe in these days.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:20.730
Inside the CDC, a brain scan shows the small section of the walker brain that still lights up,

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:25.090
allowing them to move and groan and eat, but everything else is turned off.

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:28.210
One could argue that this means they’re biologically alive

00:03:28.210 --> 00:03:29.130
Carl: "They're dead now."

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:30.570
Lizzie: "No they're not. They're just different"

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:36.030
Plenty of animals, like worms and insects, do just fine with not much more than what the walkers are working with.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:40.520
The show is constantly exploring the distinction between life and death,

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:44.160
sometimes blurring it, sometimes questioning it,  sometimes affirming it.

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:47.360
Characters are constantly unable to let go of their loved ones.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:50.580
Morgan can’t shoot his wife and eventually loses his son for it.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:55.540
Hershel goes collecting zombies like they’re Pokémon, convinced that there’s still something human about them.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:58.280
And The Governor keeps his daughter trapped in the closet.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:02.020
R. Kelly: ♫ "I'm in the closet like, 'Man, what the f**k is goin on'"? ♫

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:03.680
In the scene where Andrea’s sister dies

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:08.300
she seems to be lovingly looking into the eyes of her sister before we learn that warm embrace

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:13.160
is just the normal murdery-zombie kind of embrace. If they have trouble letting go of loved-ones

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:17.380
who have turned, it’s because the definition of life was never really clear to begin with.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:21.640
We can break up the questions of life into
the philosophical and biological senses.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:24.120
If it’s the ability to walk around and make noises

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:26.219
well, the walkers already have that covered.

00:04:26.219 --> 00:04:28.839
But there’s also the philosophical question
of life:

00:04:28.839 --> 00:04:31.419
What constitutes a life worth living?

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:36.500
Each character has their own reasons for going on with life. For Dale all life is sacred.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:42.960
Dale admits that he just can’t let go, no matter how bad things get, even when his own wife was dying from cancer.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:47.100
Dale: "After all the surgeries, chemos...she accepted it, ya know?"

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:52.160
Dale: "But I never could. And I spent, last few years, so angry."

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:57.180
Carol values survival as its own end too, but without the saccharine moralism.

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:02.920
No matter how terrible things get in her life, she just goes on surviving. She’s utterly pragmatic.

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:07.120
Secretly teaching kids how to defend themselves,  pretending to be a harmless housewife,

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:09.759
dressing up like one of the Wolves — the list goes on.

00:05:09.759 --> 00:05:12.039
And, of course, there’s this speech from
Rick:

00:05:12.039 --> 00:05:15.979
Rick: “I asked my grandpa once
if he ever killed any Germans in the war."

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:18.880
Rick: "He said he was dead the 
minute he stepped into enemy territory."

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:23.240
Rick: "And then after a few years of pretending he was dead, he made it out alive."

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:29.320
RIck: "And that's the trick of it, I think. We do what we need to do, and then we get to live."

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:33.040
The question at hand here, at the CDC, and
throughout the show seems to be:

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:35.400
What’s the point of living if we’re already dead?

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:41.100
Is the small glimmer of electric “life” inside the brains of the dead that much different than the tiny glimmer

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:44.560
of hope that allows Rick and the group to beast through this hell on Earth?

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:50.739
For Rick, being biologically alive is simply a means to an end — for the chance that one day, you’ll get to live.

00:05:50.739 --> 00:05:53.379
Mike: "Where's the happy ending here? This isn't life!"

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:59.500
According to our old friend Albert Camus, “there is but one truly philosophical problem, and that is suicide.”

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:03.680
At the CDC, Jenner, Andrea, and Jacqui decide there's no point to live a life

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:06.920
devoid of meaning and full of misery and want to end it.

00:06:06.920 --> 00:06:09.360
Andrea: "There's nothing...left."

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:15.280
But for Camus, despite the meaningless of it all, suicide isn’t liberating —&nbsp;only life offers us freedom.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:20.540
The rest of Rick’s group cling to a notion of freedom,&nbsp;not exactly Camus’ notion of freedom,&nbsp;and keep going.

00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:24.640
And then they, you know, lock themselves up in a prison for the sake of surviving.

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:27.640
Another philosophical question at hand in The Walking Dead is:

00:06:27.640 --> 00:06:32.020
What makes us human? Or, put the other way, what makes us not a zombie?

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:37.380
We could say that, unlike zombies, humans have purpose and, for one, don’t hunger for flesh.

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:41.140
Except, well, we’re literally confronted with a band of cannibals,

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:43.500
and the existential question of the show seems to be:

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:48.860
If the survivors are just aimlessly wandering around without a purpose from one source of sustenance to the next

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:53.480
and eventually have to resort to cannibalism, then what really differentiates them from the zombies?

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:56.980
This blurry distinctions between human and
animal leads to a dilemma —

00:06:56.980 --> 00:07:01.680
a dilemma that is constantly driving the survivors to distance themselves from the walkers

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:03.200
Lori: "You put me down immediately!"

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:09.360
Most survivors want to be killed so they can’t turn. If you die before you’ve turned, you get a proper burial.

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:10.900
Glenn: "We don't burn them!"

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:11.980
Glenn: "We bury them."

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:17.680
And huge parts of the second season center around Herschel’s refusal to lose empathy for his turned loved-ones.

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:24.000
It seems that everything that comes to define us as humans is stripped away, leaving only our biological selves.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:28.400
Rick is no longer a cop, but a man searching for his offspring and mating partner.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:30.980
Social distinctions are, allegedly, gone.

00:07:30.980 --> 00:07:32.600
Rick: "Look here, Merle."

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:34.100
Rick: "Things are different now."

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:37.040
Rick: "There are no 'niggers' anymore."

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:40.640
Rick: "No dumb as shit, inbred, white-trash fools either."

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:44.580
Rick: "Only dark meat and white meat. There's us and the dead."

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:48.560
Glenn used to be a delivery guy, now he’s just really good at sneaking around.

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:53.460
Still, the survivors still desperately cling to symbols that used to define their humanity.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:56.680
Symbols that, in the grand scheme of things, seem kind of stupid.

00:07:56.680 --> 00:08:01.500
Rick is constantly losing, and recovering, his sheriff’s hat before bestowing it on Carl —

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:04.120
a reminder of Rick’s past life and the law.

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:05.860
Carol misses her Maytag,

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:07.740
Carol: "I do miss my Maytag."

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:12.720
Andrea takes a Mermaid necklace, itself an amalgam of man and beast, for her sister,

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:17.260
and the show’s creators really really want
to show us how awesome hot showers are.

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:21.720
So, in other words, a big part of what separates
human from animal seems to be:

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:24.780
our social status, our stuff, and hot showers.

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:29.820
What makes humans, well, human, is one of
philosophy’s oldest questions.

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:35.420
Aristotle, for instance, argued that that our ability
to speak and reason separated us from animals,

00:08:35.420 --> 00:08:38.440
who merely had the ability to grunt base needs.

00:08:38.440 --> 00:08:41.380
Zombie: "More Brains!"

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:46.080
In the world of zombies, the very idea that a human has lost their ability to reason and speak

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:50.000
warrants the claim that they are no longer human. Then again, we could also think

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:54.940
of the small part of the brain animating the Walkers as the animal part of our own brain.

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:57.020
Part 2: Moral Lines

00:08:57.020 --> 00:09:01.320
In the world of The Walking Dead, zombies aren’t always the scariest thing out there.

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:04.400
The show is constantly exhibiting humanity’s worst:

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:05.740
Abusive partners

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:07.100
Rapists

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:08.000
Bandits

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:09.560
Whatever the hell the Wolves are doing

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:11.240
And, of course, Terminus.

00:09:11.240 --> 00:09:15.840
The fact that the home of the cannibals is
named after a Roman god isn’t a coincidence.

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:19.960
Terminus is the Roman god of boundaries, and throughout the show we are confronted with

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:25.760
oh-so-many boundaries being crossed. Not only do its denizens survive by eating human flesh,

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:29.780
but its captives are kept in train cars that evoke Nazi death camps.

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:33.520
Carol even stumbles upon a room of possessions stolen from their victims.

00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:36.600
Rick’s group is brought to the floor of an industrial slaughterhouse,

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:41.780
where Gareth’s henchman first knock out their victims and then slice their throats as if they were cattle.

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:47.540
Gareth calmly walks in with a notebook asking his henchmen what their shot counts were for accounting purposes.

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:48.680
Gareth: "What were your shot counts?"

00:09:48.680 --> 00:09:50.400
Bat Smock Man: "38"

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:55.780
We could probably make a whole episode about this scene alone, but what we've got here is a series of binaries

00:09:55.780 --> 00:09:56.820
Human — Zombie

00:09:56.820 --> 00:09:57.740
Human — Animal

00:09:57.740 --> 00:09:58.660
Good — Evil

00:09:58.660 --> 00:10:00.360
Civilization — Barbarism

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:01.560
all being blurred.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:05.200
The residents of Terminus have simultaneously
lost their humanity

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:08.540
and yet, ironically, have exhibited humanity's crowning achievement:

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:09.960
Bureaucracy

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:15.940
These boundaries function as more than just empty rhetoric —&nbsp;they’re deeply productive, for better or worse.

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:20.200
The line between human and animal serves as a framework for how people should live their lives

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:25.980
before and after the zombie apocalypse, but it also demarcates what gets screwed over in the distinction.

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:28.900
Gareth: "You're either the Butcher or the Cattle

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:31.000
Spoiler: 
It sucks to be the cattle.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:35.520
It’s why the show focuses so much on the
group’s struggle to retain their humanity.

00:10:35.530 --> 00:10:38.030
The residents of Terminus were not always like this

00:10:38.030 --> 00:10:42.350
and function as a sort of cautionary tale for surviving in the zombie apocalypse.

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:48.330
Originally an actual sanctuary for survivors, a group of bandits rolled in and proceeded to lock its inhabitants

00:10:48.330 --> 00:10:52.290
in train cars before sexually assaulting and eventually murdering them.

00:10:52.290 --> 00:10:58.300
When the original residents of Terminus successfully rebel, they lock their tormentor in a train car to rot,

00:10:58.300 --> 00:11:01.000
where he spends his time yelling, “We’re the same."

00:11:01.000 --> 00:11:07.160
It becomes clear that morality, along with a Maytag and a shower,  is what separates us from animals.

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:11.240
When escaping from Terminus, Rick wants to abandon the others trapped in the train cars

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:15.200
until Glenn convinces him that
helping other is essential to their identity.

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:17.240
Glenn: "That's still who we are."

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:18.100
"It's gotta be."

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:24.180
In the episode "Judge, Jury, and Execution,” Rick’s group is faced with a decision of what to do with a captured survivor,

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:26.620
who may or may not imperil the group if let go.

00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:30.500
Dale is convinced that the loss of justice equates
to the loss of humanity.

00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:37.460
Dale: "Now the world we know is gone, but keeping our humanity? That's a choice."

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:41.420
Even Gareth likens the signs that would lead
to his torture with his humanity.

00:11:41.420 --> 00:11:43.800
Gareth: "We were being human beings."

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:45.520
Alex: "What are we now, Gareth?

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:49.380
Other citizens of Terminus equate morals with
the inability to survive.

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:52.960
Martin: "You're a good guy."

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:54.260
"That's why you're gonna die today."

00:11:54.260 --> 00:11:58.020
Clearly, being human is more than just a set
of biological questions.

00:11:58.020 --> 00:12:02.740
What The Walking Dead really explores is that there are many, many things that define our humanity.

00:12:02.740 --> 00:12:07.440
A critical point in all this is whether or not the walkers maintain a shred of their former selves.

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:09.660
The Governor: "Let me ask you something, huh..."

00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:13.380
"Do you still believe the 'biters still have some spark in them, huh?"

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:15.380
"Of who they were?"

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:16.460
Milton: "I think so"

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:18.060
The Governor: "And that was my daughter, wasn't it?"

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:21.160
Then again, one doesn't have to "turn" to become a monster.

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:25.740
Milton: "I knew Philip —&nbsp;before he became The Governer. That man still exists."

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:30.540
Just as we question whether the undead maintain any element of their past identity, we also ask:

00:12:30.540 --> 00:12:35.240
Do those alive, but morally contaminated retain any sense of their former selves?

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:37.540
This is further explored through the character of Morgan.

00:12:37.540 --> 00:12:42.020
After his wife and son die, Morgan loses not only himself, but his connection to reality.

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:48.140
But he's revived with the help of a former psychiatrist, who teaches him that no person is too far gone to be rehabilitated.

00:12:48.140 --> 00:12:54.240
It's why Morgan refuses to kill even the Wolves, who most consider to have lost every last bit of their humanity.

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:56.240
Part 3: Dictatorship

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:58.540
A recurring theme in the show is the struggle between

00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:00.300
dictatorship and democracy.

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:02.620
As it turns out, trying to juggle finding food

00:13:02.620 --> 00:13:03.660
maintaining a shelter

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:04.780
and not getting murdered

00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:06.060
is pretty complicated.

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:10.840
Especially when multiple people are involved.

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:14.120
We see early on the complications that arise from this:

00:13:14.120 --> 00:13:15.680
Arguments about where to go

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:16.680
how to get there

00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:18.020
who needs to do what

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:20.520
and what to do when people break the rules.

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:22.100
Also, who’s in charge?

00:13:22.100 --> 00:13:24.060
While Rick’s group of survivors talk it out

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:26.160
the always-imminent backup plan is

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:27.480
sheer violence.

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:29.880
Not to mention that, 
when walkers are a-comin',

00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:32.720
talking it out isn’t always the most expedient solution.

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:36.280
When Shane and Rick can’t see eye-to-eye on anything anymore

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:37.380
both of them conclude

00:13:37.380 --> 00:13:38.800
it’s murdering time.

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:40.120
With Rick eventually winning.

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:44.040
As the show progresses, Rick is more or less
in charge.

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:46.920
But the most interesting thing happens at the end of Season 2

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:49.560
When Rick openly declares a dictatorship.

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:51.560
Rick: "This isn't a democracy anymore."

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:54.000
It’s important to note the origin of dictatorships.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:57.180
The term comes from the Ancient Roman legal convention

00:13:57.180 --> 00:14:02.380
wherein absolute power was bestowed
upon an individual in cases of extreme emergency.

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:05.280
While now the term is thrown around to indicate
shitty rulers

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:07.860
and the people we think "resemble" those shitty rulers

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:09.840
the Roman Dictatorship
was a necessity

00:14:09.840 --> 00:14:13.100
to protect against utter annihilation from invading armies.

00:14:13.100 --> 00:14:15.800
The famous good-guy-dictator
was Cincinnatus.

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:17.500
Cincinnatus was a Roman statesman

00:14:17.510 --> 00:14:20.030
who, after his son ran into some legal trouble

00:14:20.030 --> 00:14:22.660
sold most of his land and retired to a small farm.

00:14:22.660 --> 00:14:25.920
When Rome was in imminent danger from
a neighboring Italian tribe

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:29.820
the Roman consul elected Cincinnatus to the position of dictator.

00:14:29.820 --> 00:14:32.340
As legend goes, a group of senators were dispatched

00:14:32.340 --> 00:14:35.820
to tell Cincinnatus the news and found him
plowing his farm.

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:37.500
Cincinnatus put on his toga

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:38.380
headed for Rome

00:14:38.380 --> 00:14:39.620
defeated the bad guys

00:14:39.620 --> 00:14:41.980
and immediately resigned from his position as dictator.

00:14:41.980 --> 00:14:44.600
Later, Cincinnatus was elected dictator again

00:14:44.600 --> 00:14:46.600
this time to save Rome from a coup.

00:14:46.840 --> 00:14:48.940
And again, once the coup was stopped

00:14:48.940 --> 00:14:51.280
Cincinnatus resigned and went back to farming.

00:14:51.280 --> 00:14:54.200
Did we mention Rick Grimes is totally Cincinnatus?

00:14:54.200 --> 00:14:55.040
Not like, oh...

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:57.300
Rick Grimes kind of reminds us of this other guy

00:14:57.300 --> 00:14:59.740
who had absolute power in the face of scary invaders.

00:14:59.740 --> 00:15:00.640
But like, oh...

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:04.180
this guy leaves his life as a farmer to protect
against hordes of enemies

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:06.640
only to go back to farming when the threat is gone.

00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:09.620
This Roman connection is suspiciously everywhere
in the show

00:15:09.620 --> 00:15:12.700
The Governor’s Fight Club is reminiscent of gladiator battles

00:15:12.700 --> 00:15:13.900
and as previously mentioned

00:15:13.900 --> 00:15:16.020
Terminus is the Roman god of boundaries.

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:18.220
The Bible verse where Jesus rises from the dead?

00:15:18.220 --> 00:15:19.300
Romans 6:4

00:15:19.300 --> 00:15:21.520
which is scrawled on Gabriel’s wall.

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:24.560
It’s not exactly clear what the show’s
creators are doing here.

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:28.140
It could just be that Roman history is a treasure trove of material

00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:30.840
when you’re trying to think of creative ways drama could play out

00:15:30.840 --> 00:15:32.500
in a hyper-violent society

00:15:32.500 --> 00:15:34.600
always on the lookout for hordes of invaders.

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:39.240
But in doing so, The Walking Dead ends up
making some really smart commentary on politics,

00:15:39.240 --> 00:15:40.440
and specifically, sovereignty.

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:43.120
Rome, after all, the was site of both history’s most

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:46.380
extreme tyrants and foundational 
representative government.

00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:48.360
Part 4: State of Exception

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:49.640
What makes a ruler?

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:52.860
According to controversial
German political theorist Carl Schmitt

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:55.000
The sovereign is whoever makes the exception.

00:15:55.000 --> 00:15:56.620
Whether you’re living in the United States

00:15:56.630 --> 00:15:57.590
or ancient Rome

00:15:57.590 --> 00:16:00.530
the sovereign is always the
person who can discard the law

00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:02.980
to carve out exceptions to the rule.

00:16:02.980 --> 00:16:05.240
It’s kind of like your shitty hometown mayor

00:16:05.240 --> 00:16:06.860
who has the police look the other way

00:16:06.860 --> 00:16:08.420
when his relatives drink and drive

00:16:08.420 --> 00:16:09.740
or smoke crack.

00:16:09.740 --> 00:16:11.480
Mayor Ford: "Yes I have smoked cracked cocaine."
R.I.P

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:13.260
The most obvious example of this is

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:15.400
Rick “Cincinnatus” Grimes

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:18.060
who simply declares that democracy-time is over.

00:16:18.060 --> 00:16:21.320
But we also see that when Alexandria
is in imminent danger

00:16:21.320 --> 00:16:23.120
it’s Rick, not Deanna

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:24.020
who is in charge.

00:16:24.020 --> 00:16:25.620
Coral: "I know you're gonna say it's not up to you..."

00:16:25.620 --> 00:16:26.720
"but it can be."

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:29.820
The law is constantly being suspended, and
not only by Rick.

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:31.720
Shane bypasses the will of the group

00:16:31.720 --> 00:16:33.420
and kills Randall in the woods.

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:36.140
Carol kills and burns flu victims in the prison

00:16:36.140 --> 00:16:37.580
to stop it from threatening the group.

00:16:37.580 --> 00:16:39.720
In fact, Shane’s criticism of Rick seems to be

00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:42.260
heavily rooted in Rick’s ability to make the exception

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:44.020
when he won’t execute Randall.

00:16:44.020 --> 00:16:46.440
Italian philosopher Giorgo Agamben argues that

00:16:46.440 --> 00:16:49.340
the state of exception has become the law of our time.

00:16:49.340 --> 00:16:51.520
No longer an infrequent occurrence

00:16:51.520 --> 00:16:53.860
the suspension of the law permeates our society

00:16:53.860 --> 00:16:57.160
we see it in the war on terror and refugee
camps around the world.

00:16:57.160 --> 00:17:01.160
Agamben wanted to figure out just how the Nazi concentration camps could happen

00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:04.120
and it was in the state of exception 
that he found his answer.

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:06.010
But because we’re talking about The Walking Dead

00:17:06.010 --> 00:17:07.150
let’s put it this way:

00:17:07.150 --> 00:17:09.260
Rick is your typical modern sovereign.

00:17:09.270 --> 00:17:11.940
He suspends the
law to get shit done and to save people.

00:17:11.950 --> 00:17:13.760
Glen: "I told for the good of everyone."

00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:15.780
Rick: "Well I thought it best that people didn't know."

00:17:15.790 --> 00:17:18.390
Sometimes, that means Rick does really 
messed up things.

00:17:18.390 --> 00:17:20.470
The difference between Rick and Gareth is only that

00:17:20.470 --> 00:17:23.360
the suspension of law has become
permanent in Terminus.

00:17:23.360 --> 00:17:26.320
Sure, there is order, but the denizens of Terminus have thrown aside

00:17:26.320 --> 00:17:28.640
the most basic laws of human morality

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:30.260
like don’t eat people.

00:17:30.260 --> 00:17:31.240
And for Agamben

00:17:31.240 --> 00:17:33.220
the idea that all of the Rick’s of the world are only

00:17:33.220 --> 00:17:35.640
a few setbacks away from becoming Gareth

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:37.060
is terrifying.

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:38.780
The show makes it really clear why

00:17:38.780 --> 00:17:39.980
as we discussed earlier

00:17:39.980 --> 00:17:42.740
all of these atrocities are in the name of survival.

00:17:42.740 --> 00:17:44.800
The whole “you’re
either the butcher or the cattle”

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:48.120
might as well be the familiar tale of the “bird
of prey” and the “lamb”

00:17:48.120 --> 00:17:50.260
espoused by Nietzsche, who of course,

00:17:50.260 --> 00:17:53.400
the Nazis loved to
take out of context in their own propaganda.

00:17:53.400 --> 00:17:57.020
Rick is constantly invoking this
“us or them” logic.

00:17:57.020 --> 00:17:59.100
Rick: "But they can't keep up, you keep going."

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:02.920
Suggesting that what separates Rick from Gareth may not be all that much.

00:18:02.920 --> 00:18:04.320
Gareth: "Nnnooooo!"
R.I.P

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:05.480
As Agamben says:

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:10.120
"The camp is the space that opens up when the state of exception starts to become the rule."

00:18:10.120 --> 00:18:12.400
The problem with all of this, Agamben argues

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:14.660
is that whether or not atrocities are committed

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:16.500
whether or not Bob is for breakfast

00:18:16.500 --> 00:18:19.360
is dependent on the law enforcer as sovereign

00:18:19.360 --> 00:18:21.040
rather than the rule of law.

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:23.220
And while we’re not living
in a zombie apocalypse

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:25.360
that logic still rules the day.

00:18:25.360 --> 00:18:26.700
Whether it’s swine flu

00:18:26.700 --> 00:18:27.980
natural disasters

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:29.040
or ISIS

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:32.660
the ability of the government to suspend the law is always imminent.

00:18:33.120 --> 00:18:33.900
Conclusion

00:18:33.900 --> 00:18:37.000
The Walking Dead continues to be one of the
smartest shows on TV

00:18:37.000 --> 00:18:39.800
and despite being set in a fictional zombie apocalypse

00:18:39.800 --> 00:18:42.320
its questions still hit pretty close to home.

00:18:42.320 --> 00:18:44.580
Questions like: what makes life worth living?

00:18:44.580 --> 00:18:45.740
What makes us human?

00:18:45.740 --> 00:18:49.260
And in what circumstances am I allowed to resort to cannibalism, again?

00:18:49.260 --> 00:18:51.060
It’s unclear where the show will go

00:18:51.060 --> 00:18:53.740
Will Rick and his group save the world and build anew

00:18:53.740 --> 00:18:57.140
or will Rick continue to ruin every place he comes across?

00:18:57.140 --> 00:18:58.160
Or, in the end

00:18:58.160 --> 00:19:00.160
will the world need to be saved from Rick?

